The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (ctl & DMA_CTL_STATUS_OWN)
    break;
```
```c
if (handled < weight) {
    napi_complete_done(napi, handled);
    bcm4908_enet_intrs_on(enet);
}
```
Reason:
The first statement checks if the descriptor is owned by the hardware, and if so, it breaks out of the loop without processing further packets. If the hardware runs out of free descriptors, it may disable the RX ring, leading to a situation where no more packets can be received. The second statement indicates that if fewer packets than the weight were handled, it completes the NAPI polling without re-enabling the RX ring, which can result in packet loss.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
bcm4908_enet_dma_rx_ring_enable(enet, &enet->rx_ring);
```
Reason:
This statement is added to re-enable the RX ring after processing the received packets. By ensuring that the RX ring is enabled every time after handling packets, it prevents the situation where the hardware stops receiving packets due to a lack of free descriptors, thus maintaining continuous network communication.

